#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199326} :activity/id #uuid "e65e5013-8e69-5be7-a53f-8247c1f26c90" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050688 :topic/id #uuid "5f2b42f8-b227-5baa-87a7-72698f765d30" :topic/math-academy-id 4164} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199263 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198366 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198240 :question/id #uuid "7d0da02a-d686-5bb1-ab44-00286ab01783" :question/math-academy-id "e-8690"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffdd4f56-003d-5eed-a60d-ba51c4c86b51" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048162}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446816}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With Inner Critical Points"} :step/id #uuid "c833d29b-43fd-5a3a-83ae-7c9928c184c2" :step/math-academy-id 32292 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199264}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199264 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198367 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198241 :question/id #uuid "9235c493-f092-5f14-b989-be3b4548a90d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8691"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "849449fb-bb08-54a3-b8ea-9f33ddd7a3e5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446818}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Optimization Problems in Context"} :step/id #uuid "b82b3836-2ed6-522e-8a6b-e9301766e38d" :step/math-academy-id 32293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199326 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199484 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the minimum and maximum values of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2+4y^2\n$$\n\nsubject to constraint $g(x,y) = x^2+y^2 \\leq 1.$ In other words, we wish to find the global extrema of $f(x,y)$ defined on the disc of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5ac34145b3911b19eb3775a915e8f0462c78502be6d6292e7682265c86eafce7.png)\n\n  \n\nTo find the extrema, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we find the critical points of $f$ inside the domain $x^2+y^2 < 1.$\n\n- Then, we find the extreme values of $f$ subject to the constraint $x^2+y^2=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers.\n\n- Finally, we determine the maximum and minimum values by evaluating $f$ at the candidate points.\n\nRecall that the critical points of a function $f(x,y)$ are the solutions to $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf 0^T.$ Now, since the derivative of $f$ is the transpose of its gradient $\\nabla f,$ the critical points are also given by the solutions to $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0.$\n\nSo, we can find the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ Note that this point belongs to our domain.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 + y^2  = 1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 8y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(4-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm 1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4-2)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $\\left(\\pm 1, 0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 1, 0), \\qquad (0, \\pm1), \\qquad (0,0).\n$$\n\nNote that we add the critical point $(0,0)$ to the list of the candidates above.\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at these points: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\pm 1,0)$ | $(0, \\pm 1)$ | $(0, 0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum of $0$ at the point $(0, 0)$ and a maximum of $4$ at the points $(0, \\pm 1).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7cc22f9f-d1be-570d-acd7-d3f2e0af7a51" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7743 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7bbfb776-26d8-5be7-abb8-cae6bab3294c" :step/math-academy-id 44831 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199327}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199327 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198405 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198047 :question/id #uuid "fb947756-3471-59c8-b3c4-9b21a1a91557" :question/math-academy-id "e-12457"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bbc1e6a-c728-5c5a-84f8-06a92c30a023" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049390}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446817}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With No Inner Critical Points"} :step/id #uuid "0e5ab67d-1254-5853-868a-8e8a117b0032" :step/math-academy-id 44833 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199263}}] :activity/title "Optimizing Multivariable Functions Using Lagrange Multipliers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198494}]}
